# Buddhist Philosophy: The Middle Way to the End of Suffering

> The Buddha taught that suffering has a cause and therefore an end.
> His analysis of mind, causation, and the self became one of history's
> most influential philosophical systems.

## 1. The Four Noble Truths

| Truth | Claim |
| --- | --- |
| Dukkha | Life involves suffering and unsatisfactoriness |
| Samudaya | Suffering arises from craving (tanha) |
| Nirodha | Ending craving ends suffering |
| Magga | The way out is the Eightfold Path |

The Buddha called this the **Middle Way (majjhima patipada)** — between
the indulgence Charvaka praised and the extreme asceticism of the
forest renouncers.

## 2. The Eightfold Path

The path has eight factors grouped into three trainings:

| Training | Factors |
| --- | --- |
| Wisdom (panna) | Right view, right intention |
| Ethical conduct (sila) | Right speech, right action, right livelihood |
| Mental discipline (samadhi) | Right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration |

[definition]
**Dukkha** is wider than pain: it covers gross suffering, the
frustration of impermanent joys, and conditioned existence as such.
**Tanha** — craving, thirst — is its cause, because craving clings to
what cannot last.
[/definition]

## 3. Impermanence and No-Self

Three marks characterise all conditioned things: **anicca**
(impermanence), **dukkha** (unsatisfactoriness), and **anatta**
(no-self). The last is the sharpest break with the Vedic traditions:
what we call a "self" is only a stream of ever-changing physical and
mental events — the five **skandhas** (form, feeling, perception,
mental formations, consciousness). No permanent **atman** is found
within.

In the Questions of King Milinda, the monk Nagasena likens the person
to a chariot: no single part is the chariot, nor the parts apart from
their arrangement — the name applies to the whole, not to an essence.

## 4. Dependent Origination

**Dependent origination (pratityasamutpada)** is the Buddha's causal
law: this being, that becomes; with the ceasing of this, that ceases.
The classical chain has **twelve links (nidanas)**:

| # | Link | # | Link |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Ignorance (avijja) | 7 | Feeling (vedana) |
| 2 | Formations (sankhara) | 8 | Craving (tanha) |
| 3 | Consciousness (vinnana) | 9 | Clinging (upadana) |
| 4 | Name-and-form (nama-rupa) | 10 | Becoming (bhava) |
| 5 | Six senses (salayatana) | 11 | Birth (jati) |
| 6 | Contact (phassa) | 12 | Aging and death (jaramarana) |

This also answers the puzzle of **rebirth without a soul**: the dying
flame lights the next candle — the new life is neither the same person
nor a different one, just as the second flame is causally continuous
without being identical. Karma is intention, and its results follow
the causal stream.

## 5. The Three Vehicles

@diagram tree id="philosophy-buddhist-schools" layout="tree" aria="Buddhism divided into Theravada, Mahayana, and Vajrayana"
  "Buddhism" -> Theravada
  "Buddhism" -> Mahayana
  "Buddhism" -> Vajrayana
  Mahayana -> Madhyamaka
  Mahayana -> Yogacara
@/diagram

| Vehicle | Canon and language | Ideal | Region |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Theravada | Pali Tipitaka; "the elders" | Arhat — personal liberation | Sri Lanka, Southeast Asia |
| Mahayana | Sanskrit sutras | Bodhisattva — universal liberation | East Asia |
| Vajrayana | Sanskrit tantras | Tantric speed: buddhahood in one life | Tibet, Himalayas |

## 6. Nagarjuna and the Two Truths

**Nagarjuna** (c. 150–250 CE), founder of the **Madhyamaka** school,
argued from dependent origination to **shunyata (emptiness)**: since
everything arises dependent on conditions, nothing has an unconditioned
own-being (svabhava). Emptiness is not nothing — it is the absence of
independent essence.

He paired this with the doctrine of **two truths**: conventionally,
persons and tables function; ultimately, all are empty of essence —
and even emptiness itself is empty. His method was the *prasanga*,
showing every metaphysical thesis self-contradictory.

The **Yogacara** school of **Asanga and Vasubandhu** (4th century CE)
instead argued for **vijnapti-matra** — "representation-only": we know
only our mental content, and the external world is a construction of
consciousness, including a deep **storehouse consciousness**
(alaya-vijnana).

## 7. Buddhist Logic and the Councils

Buddhist epistemology culminated with **Dignaga** and **Dharmakirti**
(6th–7th centuries CE), who accepted only two pramanas — perception and
inference — and analysed meaning through **apoha** (exclusion: a word
means what it excludes, since no universal is perceived). The community
fixed its canon in a series of councils:

| Council | Place and time | Patron |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First | Rajagriha, soon after the Buddha's death | Ajatashatru |
| Second | Vaishali, c. 383 BCE | Kalasoka |
| Third | Pataliputra, c. 250 BCE | Ashoka |
| Fourth | Kashmir, 1st century CE | Kanishka |

## 8. Buddhism and the Vedic Tradition

| Issue | Vedic view | Buddhist view |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Self | Eternal atman | No permanent self (anatta) |
| Authority | The Vedas as revelation | The Buddha's verified experience and reasoning |
| Method | Ritual (karma-kanda) first | Meditation and ethics over sacrifice |
| Goal | Moksha — unity with or release of atman | Nirvana — extinguishing craving and grasping |
| Language | Sanskrit authority | Pali and local languages for teaching |

[summary]
Buddhism diagnoses suffering (dukkha), traces it to craving (tanha),
and prescribes the Eightfold Path. No-self (anatta) and impermanence
(anicca) are explained through dependent origination — rebirth without
a transmigrating soul, like flame passing to flame. Theravada,
Mahayana, and Vajrayana split over canon and ideal; Nagarjuna's
emptiness and Dharmakirti's logic carried the school to its peak.
[/summary]
